%[text] %[text:anchor:T_453a] # Estimate LLC Converter Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] This script helps you design and tune the resonant tank parameters of an LLC converter. You specify the converter ratings and constraints, sweep the gain curves to select a suitable quality factor and inductance ratio, and then verify that the final design meets all requirements.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1541] ## Overview
%[text] You design the LLC resonant tank by selecting a quality factor $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and inductance ratio $k=L\_m /L\_r${"editStyle":"visual"}. These two parameters, together with the resonant frequency and load resistance, determine most of the resonant tank component values. The key relationships are:
%[text] $\\;Q=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{L\_r }{C\_r }}}{R\_{\\textrm{ac}-\\textrm{load}} }${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  $k=\\frac{L\_{m\\;} }{L\_r }${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  $f\_{r\\;} =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\;\\sqrt{\\;L\_r C\_r }}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $n=\\frac{N\_1 }{N\_2 }${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  $R\_{\\textrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} =\\frac{8n^2 }{\\pi {\\;}^2 }\\left(\\frac{V\_o^2 }{P\_{\\textrm{load}} }\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] ### Series Resonant Inductance
%[text] You can achieve the series resonant inductance of the LLC converter either by adding an external inductor or by using the leakage inductance of the transformer. By utilizing the transformer's leakage inductance, you can reduce the size and weight of the converter. You distribute the required series resonant inductance between the primary and secondary windings using the inductance distribution factor $h${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%[text] $L\_r =L\_{\\textrm{lk1}} +n^2 L\_{\\textrm{lk2}}${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  $L\_{\\textrm{lk1}} =hL\_r${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  $L\_{\\textrm{lk2}} =\\left|\\frac{\\left(1-h\\right)L\_r }{n^2 }\\right|${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] The workflow has three steps: 
%[text] 1. Specify converter ratings and constraints
%[text] 2. Sweep gain curves to select $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and $k${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] 3. Verify the final design.  \
%[text] Additional equations and definitions are provided in the [Annexure](internal:H_annex) at the end.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_82a0] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Input your LLC converter specifications. These define the operating voltage range, power level, and resonant frequency for the design.
LLCSpec.LLCModelOption = "Full-Bridge"; % Select LLC converter model option %[control:dropdown:8cd0]{"position":[26,39]}
LLCSpec.minInputVoltage =  simscape.Value(380,"V"); % Minimum input voltage (V)
LLCSpec.maxInputVoltage =  simscape.Value(400,"V"); % Maximum input voltage (V)
LLCSpec.minOutputVoltage = simscape.Value(350,"V"); % Minimum output voltage (V)
LLCSpec.maxOutputVoltage = simscape.Value(400,"V"); % Maximum output voltage (V)

LLCSpec.ratedOutputPower = simscape.Value(10000,"W"); % Rated power (W)
LLCSpec.resonantFrequency = simscape.Value(100e3,"Hz"); % Resonant frequency (Hz)
LLCSpec.mosfetDrainSourceCapacitance = simscape.Value(200e-12,"F"); % MOSFET parasitic capacitance (F)
LLCSpec.inductanceDistributionFactor = 0.95; % Inductance distribution factor
nominalOutputVoltage = (LLCSpec.minOutputVoltage.value+LLCSpec.maxOutputVoltage.value)/2;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_mag] ## Specify Magnetizing Current Limit
%[text] The magnetizing current must be large enough for zero-voltage switching but small enough to limit conduction losses. Specify which voltage pair anchors the turns ratio, or set the ratio directly, and the maximum allowable magnetizing current as a percentage of rated input current at the resonant frequency.
%[text] To set the turns ratio yourself, set defineTurnsRatio to the ratio you want. That ratio then replaces the voltage pair, and it is how you trade gain headroom against magnetizing current: a lower ratio raises the gain the tank must supply, a higher one lowers it. An empty value derives the ratio from the voltage pair instead. Give `plotGainCurveLLCConverter` a vector of ratios to get one gain curve per ratio. The required gain range does not move with the ratio, because it comes from the voltage specification, so a ratio sweep shows the curves moving against a range that stays put.
%[text] The primary winding count anchors the integer winding pair, because the LLC transfers power in one direction. The design functions default it to $N\_1 =50${"editStyle":"visual"}, and the secondary count follows as $N\_2 =\\textrm{round}\\left(N\_1 /n\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}. The ratio set below is $n=0\\ldotp 9595${"editStyle":"visual"}, so $N\_1 =50\\to N\_2 =52${"editStyle":"visual"}, which realizes a ratio of 0.9615. Use the same ratio in every function call below, otherwise the swept tank and the designed tank describe different transformers.
  %[control:button:7cae]{"position":[1,2]}
LLCSpec.turnratioVoltageLevel = "Max Output Volttage and Min Input Voltage"; % Which voltage pair anchors the turns ratio %[control:dropdown:9819]{"position":[33,76]}
defineTurnsRatio = []; % Turns ratio of the selected design, if left empty, LLCSpec.turnratioVoltageLevel will define the turns ratio
LLCSpec.magCurrentPercentofInputCurrent = 40; % Magnetizing current limit on the percentage of rated input load current at resonant frequency
LLCSpec.minimumAllowedDeadTime = simscape.Value(200e-9,"s"); % Minimum dead time possible due to processor and switching device hardware limitation
LLCSpec.maximumAllowedDeadTime = simscape.Value(0.08/LLCSpec.resonantFrequency.value,"s"); % Maximum dead time, typically 5 to 10 % of switching PWM time
computeMagnetizingCurrentLimit(LLCSpec,TurnsRatio=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:3742173b]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_para] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
%[text] Parasitic resistances affect the gain curve and overall converter efficiency. Specify realistic values from component datasheets.
LLCSpec.Rco = 0.02; % Output filter capacitor ESR resistance (Ohm)
LLCSpec.R1 = 0.01; % Transformer primary resistance (Ohm)
LLCSpec.R2 = 0.01; % Transformer secondary resistance (Ohm)
LLCSpec.Rcr = 1e-4; % Resonant capacitor resistance (Ohm)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6293] ## Select Quality Factor and Inductance Ratio
%[text] You select the resonant tank parameters by tuning the quality factor $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and inductance ratio $k${"editStyle":"visual"} using gain curve sweeps at various load conditions. The product $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} controls how much magnetizing current flows through the resonant tank. A higher $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} reduces the magnetizing current, which lowers conduction losses but also flattens the gain curve. A flatter gain curve means the peak may not rise far enough above the required maximum gain, leaving the controller with very little room to regulate. Conversely, a lower $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} produces a taller gain peak with more control margin, but at the cost of higher magnetizing current which helps in soft-switching but increases the transformer and device conduction loss.
%[text] When sweeping $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and $k${"editStyle":"visual"} in the following subsections, check that:
%[text] - The product $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} must exceed the minimum value reported by `computeMagnetizingCurrentLimit`. This keeps the magnetizing current within the specified limit.
%[text] - The gain curve peak at full load must rise at least 5-10% above the maximum gain line. This gives the controller enough slope to regulate without large frequency swings.
%[text] - The minimum switching frequency may be expected to fall below the resonant frequency, at least at light load. Lower the turns ratio if the gain at resonance sits too close to the maximum gain.
%[text] - The maximum switching frequency at full load should stay below 1.5 to 1.6 times the resonant frequency to limit core and switching losses.
%[text] - All criteria must be satisfied at both full load and light load. Full load is the hardest case for peak margin; light load is the hardest case for frequency range. \
%[text] No single $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and $k${"editStyle":"visual"} combination satisfies all criteria perfectly. Use the sweeps below to find an acceptable compromise, adjusting the turns ratio if needed.
%[text] This workflow uses Control System Toolbox™ to plot the gain curve of the LLC converter. To open the model that plots the gain curve, in the command prompt run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] open_system("GainCurveLLCConverter");
%[text] ```
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3d2a] ### Different DC Load Resistance
%[text] Plot the gain curve for various load conditions at a fixed quality factor and inductance ratio. The gain curves should cover the entire desired gain range across all load conditions.
  %[control:button:084c]{"position":[1,2]}
qualityFactor = 1.0; % Quality factor
k = 15; % Inductance ratio

outputLoadPU = [0.3 0.6 1]; % DC output load (pu)
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(outputLoadPU*LLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm"); % Output DC resistance

gainCurve = plotGainCurveLLCConverter(LLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:4a435b0b] %[output:9ded4ec3] %[output:01dbe623]
    LmLrRatioVec=k,RloadVec=Rload,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %#ok<*NASGU> %[output:group:4a435b0b] %[output:9ded4ec3] %[output:01dbe623]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4efc] ### Different Quality Factor
%[text] Plot the gain curve for various quality factors at a fixed inductance ratio and full load. Select the quality factor that provides sufficient gain range and acceptable operating frequency.
  %[control:button:57bd]{"position":[1,2]}
qualityFactor = [0.4 0.6 0.8]; % Quality factor
k = 9; % Inductance ratio
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(LLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm");
gainCurve = plotGainCurveLLCConverter(LLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:37716750] %[output:1d3d154b] %[output:25b4f25e]
    LmLrRatioVec=k,RloadVec=Rload,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:group:37716750] %[output:1d3d154b] %[output:25b4f25e]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7667] ### Different Inductance Ratio
%[text] Using the quality factor identified above, sweep the inductance ratio at full load. Select the inductance ratio that balances peak margin with magnetizing current.
  %[control:button:6400]{"position":[1,2]}
qualityFactor = 0.5; % Quality factor
k = [5 8 11 14]; % Inductance ratio vector
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)/LLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value,"Ohm");
gainCurve = plotGainCurveLLCConverter(LLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:2b29eea9] %[output:1bfc507d] %[output:3adb7651]
    LmLrRatioVec=k,RloadVec=Rload,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:group:2b29eea9] %[output:1bfc507d] %[output:3adb7651]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_verify] ## Verify the Selected Design
%[text] After selecting $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and $k${"editStyle":"visual"} from the sweeps above, verify that the final design meets all requirements: the gain curve covers the full operating range, the component values are practical, and soft switching is maintained.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_89d4] ### Selected Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] Plot the gain curve at the selected quality factor and inductance ratio for the expected load range to confirm the operating frequency limits.
  %[control:button:3672]{"position":[1,2]}
LLCSpec.qualityFactor = 0.45; % Quality factor
LLCSpec.k = 13; % Inductance ratio
defineTurnsRatio = 0.9595; % Turns ratio of the selected design
outputLoadPU = 1; % DC output load (pu)
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(outputLoadPU*LLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm"); % Output DC resistance
[~, Frequency, frequencyRange] = plotGainCurveLLCConverter(LLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=LLCSpec.qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:85bf285c] %[output:21961b4a] %[output:3340fc46]
    LmLrRatioVec=LLCSpec.k,RloadVec=Rload,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio, ... %[output:21961b4a] %[output:3340fc46]
    FrequencyVec=linspace(0.1,3.2,150)*LLCSpec.resonantFrequency); %[output:group:85bf285c] %[output:21961b4a] %[output:3340fc46]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_500a] ### Estimate LLC Converter Parameters
%[text] Compute the resonant tank component values for the selected quality factor and inductance ratio.
[designTable, LLCDesign]  = designLLCFullBridgeConverter(LLCSpec,TurnsRatio=defineTurnsRatio); % Estimate LLC converter parameters %[output:48b098e7]
%[text] 
%[text] Verify that the selected magnetizing inductance provides soft switching over the required frequency range. The chosen magnetizing inductance should be less than the maximum value estimated by `estimateMaximumMagnetizingInductance` both near and far from the resonant frequency.
SwitchingFrequency = simscape.Value(60e3,"Hz");
magL = estimateMaximumMagnetizingInductance(LLCDesign,SwitchingFrequency=SwitchingFrequency,DisplayTableFlag=true); %[output:70b8f619]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_imped] ### Plot the Impedance Curve
%[text] Plot the impedance curve of the resonant tank to confirm the low-frequency capacitive region and the high-frequency inductive region are as expected.
  %[control:button:9810]{"position":[1,2]}
outputLoadPU = [0.4 0.6 1]; % DC output load (pu) at which impedance curve is plotted
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(outputLoadPU*LLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm"); % Output DC resistance

[magnitude, frequency] = plotLLCResonantTankImpedanceCurve(LLCDesign,RloadVec=Rload); %[output:547e0d51]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_annex] ## Annexure: Equations and Definitions
%[text] $f\_{\\textrm{r2}} =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\;\\sqrt{\\;\\left(L\_r +L\_m \\right)C\_r }}\\;${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  $x=\\frac{f\_s }{f\_r }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $L\_{m-\\max } =\\left(1-\\frac{T\_s -T\_r }{\\left(\\sqrt{\\;K+1}-1\\right)T\_r }\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\left(1+2k\\sqrt{\\;k+1}-k\\right)T\_{\\textrm{dead}} }{16\\left(k+1\\right)f\_r C\_{\\textrm{parasitic}} }\\right)\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{given}\\;\\textrm{in}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{reference}\\;\\left\\lbrack 4\\right\\rbrack${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $T\_{\\textrm{dead}\\;} \\ge 16\\;C\_{\\textrm{parasitic}} L\_m f\_s${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $K\\times Q=\\;\\frac{{\\pi \\;V}\_{\\mathrm{magL}} }{{2I}\_m R\_{\\textrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} }\\;${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text] $\\mathrm{At}\\;\\mathrm{resonant}\\;\\mathrm{frequency}\\;V\_{\\mathrm{magL}} =V\_{\\mathrm{in}-\\mathrm{avg}\\;}${"editStyle":"visual"},     $V\_{\\mathrm{in}-\\mathrm{avg}} =\\frac{V\_{\\mathrm{in}-\\min \\;} +V\_{\\mathrm{in}-\\max } }{2}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $I\_m =\\frac{V\_{\\mathrm{magL}} }{L\_m }\\frac{1}{4f\_s }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] ### Variable Definitions
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nQ-\\textrm{Quality}\\;\\textrm{factor}\\\\\nk-\\textrm{Inductance}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nf\_r -\\textrm{Resonant}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_{\\textrm{r2}} -\\textrm{Second}\\;\\textrm{resonant}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_s -\\textrm{Switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nx-\\textrm{Frequency}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nL\_m -\\textrm{Magnetizing}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\\\\nL\_{m-\\max } -\\textrm{Maximum}\\;\\;m\\textrm{agnetizing}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\\\\nT\_{\\textrm{dead}} -\\textrm{Dead}\\;\\textrm{time}\\\\\nn-\\textrm{Transformer}\\;\\textrm{turns}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nR\_{\\textrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} -\\textrm{Effective}\\;\\textrm{AC}\\;\\textrm{load}\\;\\textrm{resistance}\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%%
